## All Propagation Types

| Propagation | Has Outer TX | No Outer TX |
|---|---|---|
| `REQUIRED` | Joins existing | Creates new |
| `REQUIRES_NEW` | Creates new (suspends outer) | Creates new |
| `SUPPORTS` | Joins existing | Runs without TX |
| `NOT_SUPPORTED` | Runs without TX (suspends outer) | Runs without TX |
| `MANDATORY` | Joins existing | **Throws exception** |
| `NEVER` | **Throws exception** | Runs without TX |
| `NESTED` | Creates nested TX (savepoint) | Creates new |

## REQUIRED — Default

```java
@Transactional  // REQUIRED by default
public void processOrder(Long orderId) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
    order.setStatus(PROCESSING);
    paymentService.charge(order);  // Called in same transaction
    inventoryService.reserve(order);  // Same transaction
    // All succeed or all rollback
}
```

## REQUIRES_NEW — Independent Commit

```java
@Service
public class AuditService {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void log(String event, Long entityId) {
        // New independent transaction
        // Commits even if caller rolls back!
        auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(event, entityId, LocalDateTime.now()));
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        try {
            processPayment(order);
        } catch (PaymentException e) {
            auditService.log("PAYMENT_FAILED", order.getId());  // Commits!
            throw e;  // Main transaction rolls back
        }
    }
}
```

## NESTED — Savepoints

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
public void processLineItem(LineItem item) {
    // Creates a savepoint in the outer transaction
    // If this fails: rollback to savepoint, outer continues
    // If outer fails: rollback everything including nested
    itemService.process(item);
}
```

## MANDATORY — Enforce Caller Convention

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.MANDATORY)
public void criticalUpdate(Entity entity) {
    // MUST be called within an existing transaction
    // Throws IllegalTransactionStateException if not
    // Used to enforce "this must always be transactional"
}
```

## Production Reality

`REQUIRES_NEW` has a cost: it requires two separate database connections simultaneously — one for the outer and one for the inner transaction. Ensure your connection pool has capacity for peak concurrent `REQUIRES_NEW` calls. If your pool size is 10 and all 10 threads use REQUIRES_NEW, you need 20 connections or you'll deadlock waiting for a connection.

## Isolation Problems

| Problem | Description |
|---|---|
| **Dirty Read** | Read uncommitted data that might be rolled back |
| **Non-Repeatable Read** | Same row gives different values on second read |
| **Phantom Read** | Range query returns different rows on second execution |

## Isolation Levels vs Problems Prevented

| Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read |
|---|---|---|---|
| READ_UNCOMMITTED | ❌ Possible | ❌ Possible | ❌ Possible |
| READ_COMMITTED | ✓ Prevented | ❌ Possible | ❌ Possible |
| REPEATABLE_READ | ✓ Prevented | ✓ Prevented | ❌ Possible |
| SERIALIZABLE | ✓ Prevented | ✓ Prevented | ✓ Prevented |

## Setting Isolation in Spring

```java
// Default: uses database default (READ_COMMITTED for PostgreSQL)
@Transactional

// Explicit isolation level
@Transactional(isolation = Isolation.SERIALIZABLE)
public void transferFunds(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) {
    Account from = accountRepo.findById(fromId).orElseThrow();
    Account to = accountRepo.findById(toId).orElseThrow();
    // No concurrent modification allowed for these accounts
    from.debit(amount);
    to.credit(amount);
}

// Read-only transactions — often lower isolation is fine
@Transactional(readOnly = true, isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED)
public ReportData generateReport() { ... }
```

## Non-Repeatable Read Example

```java
// Transaction A — READ_COMMITTED
Order order1 = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();  // status = PENDING
// Transaction B commits: UPDATE orders SET status = 'CONFIRMED' WHERE id = 1
Order order2 = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();  // status = CONFIRMED
// order1.getStatus() != order2.getStatus() — non-repeatable!

// With REPEATABLE_READ: order2 still reads PENDING within same transaction
```

## Production Reality

Don't change isolation levels without understanding the performance implications. `SERIALIZABLE` can cause significant lock contention and timeouts under concurrent load. PostgreSQL's `SERIALIZABLE` uses Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) which is more concurrent than traditional locking, but still has overhead. Profile before and after changing isolation levels.

## Performance Impact

Hibernate's dirty checking process:
1. Load entity → take snapshot of all fields
2. At flush: compare current state to snapshot for every managed entity
3. For changed entities: generate UPDATE SQL

With `readOnly = true`:
- No snapshots taken → less memory
- No dirty checking → less CPU
- FlushMode set to NEVER → no accidental flushes

## Usage

```java
@Service
@Transactional  // Default for write operations
public class OrderService {

    // Read operations — optimize with readOnly
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Order findById(Long id) {
        return orderRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Page<OrderSummary> getOrderPage(Pageable pageable) {
        return orderRepository.findAllSummaries(pageable);
    }

    // Write operations — no readOnly
    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        return orderRepository.save(new Order(req));
    }
}
```

## Class-Level Default + Method Override Pattern

```java
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)  // Default for all methods: read-only
public class ProductService {

    public List<Product> findAll() { ... }         // read-only ✓
    public Product findById(Long id) { ... }       // read-only ✓

    @Transactional  // Override: write transaction
    public Product create(ProductRequest req) { ... }

    @Transactional  // Override: write transaction
    public Product update(Long id, ProductRequest req) { ... }
}
```

## Spring Data Repository Default

Spring Data JPA's `SimpleJpaRepository` follows this pattern — all query methods are `readOnly = true`, all write methods (`save`, `delete`) are `readOnly = false`.

## Production Reality

Apply `readOnly = true` consistently on all query service methods. For large reporting transactions that load thousands of entities, the memory saving from skipped snapshots is significant. Also useful when using read replicas — your routing DataSource can detect `readOnly = true` and route to the replica automatically.

## Default Behavior

```java
@Transactional
public void processOrder(Order order) throws Exception {
    orderRepo.save(order);  // Saved to DB

    // RuntimeException → ROLLBACK (default)
    if (validation.fails()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid order");

    // Checked Exception → COMMIT (!)
    notificationService.send(order);  // throws IOException
    // order IS committed even though IOException was thrown!
}
```

## Customizing Rollback

```java
// Rollback on any exception (most common override)
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void processOrderStrict(Order order) throws IOException {
    orderRepo.save(order);
    notificationService.send(order);  // IOException → ROLLBACK
}

// Rollback on specific checked exception only
@Transactional(rollbackFor = OrderValidationException.class)
public void processOrderCustom(Order order) throws OrderValidationException {
    orderRepo.save(order);
}

// Don't rollback on specific RuntimeException
@Transactional(noRollbackFor = OutOfStockWarningException.class)
public void fulfillOrder(Order order) {
    order.setStatus(FULFILLED);
    try {
        inventoryService.deduct(order);
    } catch (OutOfStockWarningException e) {
        // Warning logged but order still committed
        log.warn("Low stock warning: {}", e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

## Programmatic Rollback

```java
@Transactional
public void processWithConditionalRollback(Order order) {
    orderRepo.save(order);
    PaymentResult result = paymentService.charge(order);

    if (!result.isSuccessful()) {
        // Mark transaction for rollback without throwing
        TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly();
        log.warn("Payment failed — rolling back order {}", order.getId());
    }
}
```

## Rollback and Exception Visibility

```java
@Transactional
public void outerMethod() {
    innerService.innerMethod();  // Throws RuntimeException → marks TX for rollback
    // Even if you catch the exception here, TX is already marked for rollback!
    // Transaction WILL rollback when outerMethod exits
}

// To allow inner method to fail without rollback:
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void innerMethod() { ... }  // Own transaction — rollback is isolated
```

## Production Reality

Always add `rollbackFor = Exception.class` to your `@Transactional` methods unless you have a specific reason for the checked-exception commit behaviour. The default is a historical decision from the EJB era and catches many developers off guard. Most Spring applications benefit from uniform rollback on any exception.

## Configuration

```java
// Timeout in seconds
@Transactional(timeout = 30)  // 30 seconds
public Order processOrder(Long orderId) {
    // Must complete within 30 seconds or TransactionTimedOutException is thrown
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();
    paymentService.charge(order);  // If this blocks for 31s → timeout!
    return orderRepo.save(order);
}

// Read-only with timeout
@Transactional(readOnly = true, timeout = 10)
public ReportData generateReport(ReportFilter filter) {
    // Complex query must finish within 10 seconds
}
```

## What Happens on Timeout

```
1. Transaction starts
2. 30 seconds pass
3. Spring marks transaction as timed out
4. Next JPA operation or flush: TransactionTimedOutException thrown
5. Transaction rolled back
6. Database connection released
7. Exception propagates to caller
```

## Global Timeout Configuration

```yaml
# application.yml (Spring Boot 3.1+)
spring:
  transaction:
    default-timeout: 30s
```

```java
// Via TransactionTemplate for programmatic use
@Bean
public TransactionTemplate transactionTemplate(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
    TransactionTemplate template = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
    template.setTimeout(30);
    return template;
}
```

## Query-Level Timeout vs Transaction Timeout

```java
// JDBC query timeout — cancels the specific query
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "5000"))
public List<Order> findSlowOrders();  // Cancels query after 5000ms

// Transaction timeout — cancels the entire transaction
@Transactional(timeout = 30)  // 30 seconds for the whole transaction
```

## Production Reality

Always set transaction timeouts for user-facing operations. A runaway query that causes a transaction to hold a connection for 10 minutes under load will exhaust your connection pool and cause cascading failures. Set the timeout at the `@Transactional` level as a safety net, and also set query-level timeouts for known expensive queries.

## Lock Mode Types

| LockModeType | SQL Generated | Allows Concurrent Reads | Allows Concurrent Writes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `PESSIMISTIC_READ` | `SELECT ... FOR SHARE` | Yes | No |
| `PESSIMISTIC_WRITE` | `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` | No (with lock) | No |
| `PESSIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT` | `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` + version bump | No | No |

## In Repository

```java
public interface SeatRepository extends JpaRepository<Seat, Long> {

    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
    @Query("SELECT s FROM Seat s WHERE s.id = :id")
    Optional<Seat> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
}

@Transactional
public void bookSeat(Long seatId, Long userId) {
    // Locks the row — other transactions block until this commits
    Seat seat = seatRepo.findByIdForUpdate(seatId).orElseThrow();

    if (seat.isBooked()) {
        throw new SeatAlreadyBookedException(seatId);
    }

    seat.book(userId);
    // Transaction commits → lock released
}
```

## In EntityManager

```java
@Transactional
public void processWithLock(Long id) {
    Entity entity = em.find(Entity.class, id, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);
    // Or lock after finding:
    em.lock(entity, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);

    // Now modify
    entity.setStatus(PROCESSING);
}
```

## Deadlock Prevention

```java
// Deadlock scenario:
// TX A locks seat 1, then tries to lock seat 2
// TX B locks seat 2, then tries to lock seat 1
// → Both wait forever!

// Prevention: always lock in a consistent order
public void swapSeats(Long seatId1, Long seatId2, Long userId) {
    Long firstLock = Math.min(seatId1, seatId2);
    Long secondLock = Math.max(seatId1, seatId2);
    // Always lock lower ID first — consistent ordering prevents deadlock
    Seat seat1 = seatRepo.findByIdForUpdate(firstLock).orElseThrow();
    Seat seat2 = seatRepo.findByIdForUpdate(secondLock).orElseThrow();
}
```

## Production Reality

For seat booking, inventory reservation, or any limited-resource allocation where the resource is contended, pessimistic locking gives deterministic behaviour. For general business data, optimistic locking is better. Monitor your database's lock wait metrics — high lock wait times indicate lock contention that may require schema design changes (finer-grained locking, queue-based processing, or partitioning).

## TransactionTemplate — Preferred Programmatic Approach

```java
@Service
public class BatchProcessor {
    private final TransactionTemplate transactionTemplate;
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    public BatchProcessor(PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
                          OrderRepository orderRepository) {
        this.transactionTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.transactionTemplate.setTimeout(30);
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
    }

    public void processBatch(List<OrderData> batchData) {
        // Commit every 100 records
        Lists.partition(batchData, 100).forEach(chunk -> {
            transactionTemplate.execute(status -> {
                chunk.forEach(data -> {
                    try {
                        orderRepository.save(new Order(data));
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        status.setRollbackOnly();  // Rollback this chunk
                        log.error("Failed chunk: {}", e.getMessage());
                    }
                });
                return null;  // Return null for void result
            });
        });
    }
}
```

## PlatformTransactionManager — Low Level

```java
@Autowired
private PlatformTransactionManager txManager;

public void manualTransaction() {
    DefaultTransactionDefinition def = new DefaultTransactionDefinition();
    def.setIsolationLevel(TransactionDefinition.ISOLATION_READ_COMMITTED);
    def.setPropagationBehavior(TransactionDefinition.PROPAGATION_REQUIRED);
    def.setTimeout(30);

    TransactionStatus status = txManager.getTransaction(def);
    try {
        // Business logic
        orderRepository.save(order);
        txManager.commit(status);
    } catch (Exception ex) {
        txManager.rollback(status);
        throw ex;
    }
}
```

## When to Use Each

| Scenario | Recommended Approach |
|---|---|
| Regular service methods | `@Transactional` |
| Dynamic transaction boundaries | `TransactionTemplate` |
| Batch processing with chunk commits | `TransactionTemplate` |
| Non-Spring-managed code | `TransactionTemplate` |
| Very specific transaction control | `PlatformTransactionManager` |

## Production Reality

`TransactionTemplate` is underused. It's particularly valuable in batch processing where you want to commit every N records to avoid long-running transactions that hold connections and consume memory. Instead of one transaction for 100,000 records, use chunks of 1,000 with a `TransactionTemplate` in a loop — same logical operation, far better resource management.

## @EventListener — Runs in Publisher's Transaction

```java
@EventListener
public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
    // Runs in OrderService's transaction
    // If called from within @Transactional:
    //   - Runs BEFORE the transaction commits
    //   - If exception thrown here: OrderService's TX rolls back
    //   - If OrderService TX rolls back: this ran for nothing
    emailService.sendConfirmation(event.getUserEmail());  // RISKY!
}
```

## @TransactionalEventListener — Runs After Commit

```java
@Component
public class OrderSideEffectListener {

    // Fires only after the creating transaction commits
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void sendConfirmationEmail(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        // Order IS in the database — safe to send email
        emailService.sendOrderConfirmation(event.getUserEmail(), event.getOrderId());
    }

    // React to order placement failure
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_ROLLBACK)
    public void handleOrderFailed(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        log.error("Order placement failed for user {}", event.getUserEmail());
        alertService.notifyOps(event);
    }

    // Always fires — after commit or rollback
    @TransactionEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMPLETION)
    public void cleanup(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        cache.evict("pendingOrders:" + event.getUserId());
    }
}
```

## Publishing the Event

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        Order order = orderRepository.save(new Order(req));
        // Event published within transaction
        publisher.publishEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(order.getId(), req.getUserEmail()));
        return order;
        // On commit: @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) fires
        // On rollback: @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_ROLLBACK) fires
    }
}
```

## Async Execution

```java
// Run the side effect asynchronously after commit
@Async
@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
public void sendEmailAsync(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
    emailService.send(...);
}
```

## Production Reality

Always use `@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)` for external side effects — emails, webhooks, SMS, payment notifications. Use `@EventListener` only for in-transaction business logic that must be part of the same atomic unit. The discipline to separate transactional business operations from post-commit side effects makes your system more consistent and easier to reason about.

## OSIV Problem

```
With spring.jpa.open-in-view=true (default):

HTTP Request arrives
  → Hibernate session opened, TX started
    → Controller called
      → Service layer: business logic
        → @Transactional methods run
      → Returns data to controller
    → Controller serializes to JSON  ← lazy loading happens HERE
  → Response sent, session closed, TX committed

Problems:
  1. DB connection held during JSON serialization
  2. N+1 queries triggered during serialization
  3. @Transactional commits but connection stays open
```

## Disable OSIV

```properties
spring.jpa.open-in-view=false
```

Now you must load everything inside `@Transactional` methods:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public OrderDetailDTO getOrderDetail(Long id) {
        // Load everything needed here — lazy loading outside TX will fail!
        Order order = orderRepo.findByIdWithItemsAndUser(id).orElseThrow();
        return orderMapper.toDetailDTO(order);  // All data loaded in-transaction
    }
}
```

## Batch Processing — Chunk Transactions

```java
@Service
public class OrderMigrationService {

    private final TransactionTemplate transactionTemplate;

    // WRONG — one giant transaction for 1M records
    @Transactional
    public void migrateBad() {
        orderRepository.findAll().forEach(this::migrate);  // Holds connection for HOURS
    }

    // CORRECT — chunk into small transactions
    public void migrateGood() {
        int page = 0;
        Page<Order> chunk;
        do {
            final int currentPage = page;
            chunk = transactionTemplate.execute(status -> {
                Page<Order> orders = orderRepository.findAll(
                    PageRequest.of(currentPage, 1000));
                orders.forEach(this::migrate);
                return orders;
            });
            page++;
        } while (chunk != null && chunk.hasNext());
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Disabling OSIV requires upfront work — you must explicitly load all required data within transactions. This discipline pays off: your service layer is self-contained, queries are predictable, and the controller only receives ready-to-serialize DTOs. With OSIV off, you'll also discover all your N+1 problems during development instead of in production.
